docs/guide/audio.md:643:- **It corrects drift.** If two clocks run at slightly different rates, the recorder slowly falls out of sync over a long take. When the clips overlap for at least 60 seconds, Frame Job measures that drift and corrects it by playing the clip at a slightly different speed, at most 0.2 % from ×1. The correction is a plain resample, so the pitch moves very slightly with it. Drift is left alone when it is under 5 ms over the overlap, when it is too small to tell from no drift (under three times its own uncertainty), or when the speed it needs is more than 0.2 % from ×1. The corrected speed appears in the clip's Speed section (for example ×1.0004), with Keep pitch turned off. The command shows no drift figure of its own; that speed is where the correction shows. Like any clip at a speed other than ×1 with Keep pitch off, the corrected clip's sound is rendered in the background, so it is briefly silent in the preview after the correction, and again after each trim, split or speed change of it (see [Speed](speed.md)); an export waits for it.
docs/guide/export.md:3:Frame Job writes the timeline out as a movie, an audio file or an animated image, and saves single frames as stills. One export sheet covers every format. You choose the format, then the size, frame rate, quality, loudness and range, and the sheet shows what the file will be before anything is written. Exports can run at once behind a progress sheet, or wait in the Export Queue while you keep editing. Every export of a file with sound reports what that sound measured.
docs/guide/export.md:47:| HEVC with Alpha (MOV) | `.mov` | A small delivery file that keeps a transparent canvas, for laying over another background. 8-bit. |
docs/guide/export.md:52:| VP9 with Alpha (WebM) | `.webm` | VP9 (WebM) that keeps a transparent canvas, for a title or a lower third a web page plays over its own background in Chrome or Firefox. 8-bit, always the SDR picture. Safari's transparent movie is HEVC with Alpha. |
docs/guide/export.md:285:A canvas whose background is transparent (the **Background** row in the Document tab's Appearance section; see [Projects and media](projects-and-media.md#appearance)) keeps its transparency only in **ProRes 4444**, **HEVC with Alpha**, **VP9 with Alpha (WebM)** and **Animated PNG**. When the format carries an alpha channel, the footnote ends in "alpha". Every other format composites the picture onto black. A GIF can show a pixel as fully transparent but has no partial transparency, so it is not one of the four.
docs/guide/export.md:295:While the canvas background is transparent, the Document tab says how many formats will keep it: "Transparent — kept by 4 export formats." These are the four formats above.
docs/guide/editing.md:163:**Paste** keeps the copied arrangement. The earliest clip lands at the playhead and the others keep their distance from it. The copied front-most lane goes onto the front track, the lanes behind it onto the tracks behind, and tracks are added at the back if there aren't enough. A pasted clip that would break the overlap rule goes on a new track at the front, so a paste is never refused for lack of room. You can paste into another project. Media is matched by file path and added to the project when it isn't there yet. Pasted clips get new identities and are snapped to the project's frame rate.
